## Why This Is Asked
Tests end-to-end design of an AWS AI pipeline handling privacy, scaling, and cost. Combines managed AI services (Comprehend, SageMaker) with event-driven wiring (S3, Lambda, DynamoDB).

## Key Concepts
- Event-driven architecture across S3, Lambda, and SageMaker
- AI services integration: Comprehend for NER/sentiment, SageMaker for topic modeling
- Data privacy: PII redaction, encryption at rest/in transit, access controls
- Cost awareness: batching, cold starts, endpoint usage, and streaming vs. per-file processing

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code: demonstrate sequence of calls to AWS services
const AWS = require('aws-sdk');
const comprehend = new AWS.Comprehend();
const sage = new AWS.SageMakerRuntime();
async function analyze(text){
  const entities = await comprehend.detectEntities({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const sentiment = await comprehend.detectSentiment({Text: text, LanguageCode:'en'}).promise();
  const res = await sage.invokeEndpoint({EndpointName:'topic-model', Body: JSON.stringify({text}), ContentType:'application/json'}).promise();
  return {entities, sentiment, topics: JSON.parse(res.Body.toString())};
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle partial failures and retries across Lambda, SageMaker, and DynamoDB?
- What security controls and monitoring would you add for regulatory compliance (e.g., encryption, audit logs, IAM least privilege)?

## Why This Is Asked

This asks for a practical, beginner-friendly AWS AI/ML workflow that combines text detection, translation, sentiment, storage, and a lightweight read API, while emphasizing security and cost.

## Key Concepts

- DetectDominantLanguage (Comprehend)
- TranslateText (Translate)
- DetectSentiment (Comprehend)
- S3 → Lambda → Step Functions orchestration
- DynamoDB indexing by documentId
- API Gateway + Lambda for reads
- IAM least privilege and KMS
- Privacy: PII masking, encryption, and retention

## Code Example

```javascript
import { ComprehendClient, DetectDominantLanguageCommand, DetectSentimentCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-comprehend";
import { TranslateClient, TranslateTextCommand } from "@aws-sdk/client-translate";

const comprehend = new ComprehendClient({region:"us-east-1"});
const translate = new TranslateClient({region:"us-east-1"});

async function analyzeText(text){
  const dl = await comprehend.send(new DetectDominantLanguageCommand({Text: text}));
  const langCode = (dl.Languages?.[0]?.LanguageCode) ?? "en";
  let english = text;
  if (langCode !== "en"){
    const tr = await translate.send(new TranslateTextCommand({Text: text, SourceLanguageCode: langCode, TargetLanguageCode: "en"}));
    english = tr.TranslatedText;
  }
  const sentiment = await comprehend.send(new DetectSentimentCommand({Text: english, LanguageCode: "en"}));
  return { language: langCode, translated: english, sentiment: sentiment.Sentiment };
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test this with mocks and local development?
- What are the implications for cost at scale and how would you mitigate?
- How would you handle a batch of emails arriving concurrently?

